You help a learner of {{ target_language_name }} who already speaks {{ known_language_name }}.
Each pair below is a {{ target_language_name }} word and a {{ known_language_name }} word that
look alike. Decide, for EACH pair, how the learner should treat the resemblance. Judge
by spelling and by how the words are used today in the standard language (Brazilian
Portuguese for Portuguese), not by etymology: what matters is whether a learner seeing
the {{ target_language_name }} word would think of the {{ known_language_name }} one.

Compare the MAIN everyday meaning of each word, the one a dictionary lists first and a
native speaker thinks of:

- "cognate": the main everyday meanings are the same, even if one word also has extra
  senses (pt "nação" / en "nation"; pt "história" / en "history").
- "partial": they share a real meaning, but the main everyday meaning of ONE of them is
  something else (pt "assistir" is mostly to watch, only formally to assist; pt
  "particular" is mostly private).
- "full": no everyday meaning in common, so the resemblance misleads (pt "esquisito" is
  weird, en "exquisite" is refined; pt "puxar" is pull, en "push" the opposite).
- "unrelated": ONLY when the spellings differ noticeably and the resemblance is a
  coincidence no learner would fall for (pt "casa" / en "cast", pt "feia" / en
  "festival").

A pair spelled identically or almost identically (the same word, or a regular ending
apart: pt "data" / en "data", pt "pato" / it "patto") is never "unrelated": if the
meanings differ it is "full" or "partial", whatever the words' origins.

The senses listed are hints from a dictionary: they can be incomplete or wrong, and
your knowledge of the words wins.

For "full" and "partial", write `note`: one short English line saying what each word
means, for example: pt "esquisito" means weird or odd; en "exquisite" is "requintado".
For "cognate" and "unrelated", leave `note` empty.

